{"qid":"Q1061488","names":["United States of Colombia"],"title":"United States of Colombia","endonym":null,"capsule":"The United States of Colombia was the federal republic established by the Rionegro Constitution of 1863, which replaced the centralist Granadine Confederation and granted near-sovereign autonomy to its nine constituent states. Among the most liberal charters in the nineteenth-century Americas, it abolished capital punishment and guaranteed wide freedoms of press, association, and religion, while drastically weakening the central executive—limited to short, non-renewable presidential terms. This radical federalism produced chronic instability: weak central revenues, recurring regional civil wars, and frequent changes of government marked the union's early years.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The political extent is identical to modern Colombia minus Panama; internal state boundaries were constitutionally defined and are precisely documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"United States of Colombia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera","nativeName":"Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera","roman":"Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera","start":1863,"end":1864,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Manuel Murillo Toro","nativeName":"Manuel Murillo Toro","roman":"Manuel Murillo Toro","start":1864,"end":1866,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera (2nd term)","nativeName":"Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera","roman":"Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera","start":1866,"end":1867,"i